WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
A Git repository is not automatically trusted just because it can be cloned.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:19.320
Automation should distinguish approved repositories from arbitrary URLs, trusted branches from untrusted branches, protected tags from user-created tags, and internal pull requests from forked or external contributions.

3
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:24.540
Each source can carry a different level of review, ownership, and operational authority.

4
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:35.380
Change control matters because Git automation often drives builds, releases, deployments, documentation publishing, artifact generation, and infrastructure workflows.

5
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:42.040
A branch that only runs validation does not need the same trust as a protected production release branch.

6
00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:50.860
A pull request from a fork should not automatically receive the same secrets or deployment authority as a reviewed merge into a protected branch.

7
00:00:51.140 --> 00:01:05.540
Protected branches, code owners, required reviews, status checks, signed or reviewed release tags where appropriate, and protected environments all help ensure that privileged automation runs only after the right level of review.

8
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.440
These controls create a path from proposed change to trusted automation.

9
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:18.860
The path should be visible enough that teams can answer what changed, who reviewed it, which ref was used, and why the automation was allowed to proceed.

10
00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.540
Production boundaries should be explicit.

11
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:28.840
A script should not deploy, publish, or mutate protected resources merely because a file changed somewhere in the repository.

12
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:34.140
Release and deployment refs need stronger governance than ordinary development refs.

13
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:43.600
The safer pattern is to make high-impact automation depend on protected branches, protected tags, approvals, or environment gates that match the organization's release process.

14
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:50.740
Teams can identify what changed, who reviewed it, which ref was used, and why the automation was allowed to proceed.
